        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "How much does it hold?"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A tank, a test tube or an ice-cream cone with ice cream on top: volumes of combined solids tell us how much they hold."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "1 litre = 1000 cm^3",
          "add volumes",
          "capsule volume",
          "cone + cylinder",
          "displacement",
          "draw and mark",
          "melting and recasting",
          "volume formulas"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Volume formulas: cuboid l b h, cylinder pi r squared h, cone one-third pi r squared h, sphere four-thirds pi r cubed, hemisphere two-thirds pi r cubed.\nFor combined solids, add the volumes of the parts. Joining does not hide any volume.\nA cone on a cylinder: V = pi r squared h(cylinder) + one-third pi r squared h(cone).\nA capsule: V = pi r squared h + two-thirds pi r cubed x 2 for both hemispheres.\nIf a solid is melted and recast, the volume stays the same: V(old) = V(new) x number of pieces.\nWhen a solid is dropped into water, the water rises by the volume of the solid.\nConvert units carefully: 1 litre = 1000 cubic centimetres.\nDraw the solid, mark the radii and heights, and then compute each part."
        }
      },
